cat > .context/decisions.md << 'EOF' # Decision Records This file tracks all major architectural and technical decisions made during project planning and development. --- ## DR-001: Target Audience & Scope **Date:** 2025-11-13 (PHASE 2: Interview) **Status:** Accepted **Context:** Ground Control can target different user segments (solo developers, enterprises, teams). Need to define primary audience to guide feature prioritization and UX complexity. **Decision:** Primary target: Solo developers and small teams (2-5 people) Secondary: Open source project maintainers, indie hackers **Rationale:** - Largest addressable market in developer tools space - Simpler collaboration features (vs enterprise) - Focus on individual productivity and learning - Aligns with UPMT's markdown-first philosophy **Consequences:** - ✅ Simpler permission model (repo owner + collaborators) - ✅ Can defer complex team features (RBAC, SSO) - ✅ Focus on educational content (Vibe Wizard) - ⚠️ May need enterprise features in v2.0+ --- ## DR-002: Deployment Strategy **Date:** 2025-11-13 (PHASE 2: Interview) **Status:** Accepted **Context:** Users value zero vendor lock-in. Need to balance SaaS convenience with self-hosting flexibility. **Decision:** Hybrid deployment model: - Primary: Vercel-hosted SaaS (groundcontrol.app) - Alternative: Docker self-hosted option **Rationale:** - SaaS: Fast onboarding, no maintenance, always up-to-date - Self-hosted: Data sovereignty, customization, air-gapped environments - Docker: One-command deployment, platform-independent - All data in GitHub (markdown/YAML) - portable between deployments **Consequences:** - ✅ Reaches both audiences (convenience seekers + privacy-conscious) - ✅ Data portability prevents lock-in - ⚠️ Need to maintain Docker deployment path - ⚠️ Configuration management for self-hosted --- ## DR-003: State Management - Zustand over Jotai **Date:** 2025-11-13 (PHASE 3: Tech Verification) **Status:** Accepted **Context:** Need lightweight state management for React. Evaluated Zustand vs Jotai as modern alternatives to Redux/Context API. **Decision:** Use Zustand 4.5.0 for global state management **Rationale:** - Simpler API: Store-based approach easier for solo developers - Better DX: Less boilerplate than Redux/Jotai - Performance: Minimal re-renders, selector-based updates - Bundle size: 1.2KB vs Jotai's 2.8KB - Active maintenance: 42K+ stars, regular updates - Team experience: More familiar pattern **Alternatives Considered:** - ❌ Jotai: Atomic approach powerful but steeper learning curve - ❌ Redux Toolkit: Too heavy for this project scope - ❌ Context API: Performance issues for frequent updates **Consequences:** - ✅ Faster development velocity - ✅ Easier onboarding for new contributors - ⚠️ Need migration path if atomic state needed later --- ## DR-004: Testing Framework - Vitest over Jest **Date:** 2025-11-13 (PHASE 3: Tech Verification) **Status:** Accepted **Context:** Need fast, reliable test runner for unit/integration tests. Jest is standard but Vitest offers modern improvements. **Decision:** Use Vitest ^2.x.x for unit/integration testing **Rationale:** - Speed: 3-8x faster than Jest (native ESM, no transpilation) - Vite integration: Reuses existing build config - Jest-compatible API: Easy migration, familiar syntax - Modern: Built for ESM-first projects - Active development: Backed by Vite ecosystem - Watch mode: Instant feedback during development **Alternatives Considered:** - ❌ Jest: Slower, CJS-based, requires config duplication - ❌ Node test runner: Less mature, fewer integrations **Consequences:** - ✅ Faster CI/CD pipelines - ✅ Better developer experience (instant test feedback) - ✅ Unified config with Vite - ⚠️ Newer tool, fewer resources/examples vs Jest --- ## DR-005: Package Manager - pnpm over npm/yarn **Date:** 2025-11-13 (PHASE 3: Tech Verification) **Status:** Accepted **Context:** Need efficient package manager for monorepo-ready project with 40+ dependencies. **Decision:** Use pnpm ^9.x.x as primary package manager **Rationale:** - Speed: Fastest install times (content-addressable store) - Disk efficiency: Saves ~40% space via symlinks - Strict: Prevents phantom dependencies - Monorepo support: Native workspaces (future-proofing) - Security: Flat node_modules prevents nested dependency issues - Industry adoption: Vercel, Vue 3, Prisma use pnpm **Alternatives Considered:** - ❌ npm: Slower, uses more disk space - ❌ yarn: Better than npm but slower than pnpm - ❌ yarn berry: Plug'n'Play complexity not justified **Consequences:** - ✅ Faster CI builds (~30% time savings) - ✅ Reduced storage costs in CI/CD - ✅ Better dependency hygiene - ⚠️ Contributors need to install pnpm (one-time setup) --- ## DR-006: Forms Library - React Hook Form over Formik **Date:** 2025-11-13 (PHASE 3: Tech Verification) **Status:** Accepted **Context:** Ground Control has many forms (PRD editor, module requirements, settings). Need performant, DX-friendly form solution. **Decision:** Use React Hook Form for all form handling **Rationale:** - Performance: Uncontrolled inputs, minimal re-renders - DX: Less boilerplate than Formik - Bundle size: 8.6KB vs Formik's 13KB - Validation: Integrates with Zod/Yup schemas - DevTools: React Hook Form DevTools for debugging - TypeScript: Excellent type inference **Alternatives Considered:** - ❌ Formik: More re-renders, larger bundle, slower development - ❌ Native forms: Too much boilerplate for complex validation **Consequences:** - ✅ Better UX (faster forms, instant validation) - ✅ Less code to maintain - ✅ Easier to build complex multi-step wizards (Vibe Wizard) - ⚠️ Need to learn uncontrolled input patterns --- ## DR-007: Implementation Phases - 6-Phase Approach **Date:** 2025-11-13 (PHASE 2: Interview + PHASE 4: Synthesis) **Status:** Accepted **Context:** 221 functions across 18 modules is massive scope. Need structured implementation plan. **Decision:** Implement in 6 phases over 18+ weeks: 1. Foundation (Weeks 1-3): Auth, Dashboard, GitHub 2. Core Features (Weeks 4-7): Editors, Progress, Vibe Wizard 3. Intelligence Layer (Weeks 8-10): AI automation, Testing 4. Advanced Features (Weeks 11-13): Backend Docs, UI Canvas 5. Enhancements (Weeks 14-16): Rules Monitor, Projects Management 6. Polish - v2.0 (Weeks 17-18+): Design System, Export, Customization **Rationale:** - Sequential dependencies (auth → dashboard → features) - MVP-focused (Phases 1-3 = usable product) - Parallel work possible within phases - Feedback loops between phases - Defer nice-to-have to v2.0 **Consequences:** - ✅ Clear milestones for tracking progress - ✅ Can release MVP after Phase 3 (~10 weeks) - ✅ Reduces risk of scope creep - ⚠️ Long timeline (4+ months to full feature set) --- ## DR-008: AI/ML Strategy - Placeholder with Future Integration **Date:** 2025-11-13 (PHASE 1: Analysis) **Status:** Accepted **Context:** Bootstrap Engine (Module 17) has 20 AI/ML functions (NLP, BERT, GPT integration). Full implementation complex and costly. **Decision:** MVP: Use placeholder/mock for AI features v2.0: Integrate real AI models (Claude API, OpenAI, or self-hosted) **Rationale:** - Focus on core CRUD and visualization features first - AI features are enhancements, not blockers - High cost of AI API calls (need revenue first) - User can manually do bootstrap initially - Allows testing UX flows without AI complexity **Consequences:** - ✅ Faster MVP delivery - ✅ Lower initial costs - ✅ Can validate product-market fit without AI - ⚠️ Need clear UX for "coming soon" AI features - ⚠️ Must design architecture to allow AI integration later --- ## Template for Future Decisions ```markdown ## DR-XXX: [Short Title] **Date:** YYYY-MM-DD **Status:** [Proposed/Accepted/Deprecated/Superseded] **Context:** [What is the issue we're seeing that motivates this decision?] **Decision:** [What is the change we're proposing/making?] **Rationale:** - [Why this option over alternatives?] - [Key benefits] **Alternatives Considered:** - ❌ Option A: [Why